# Molecule D is the only molecule required for growth. Strains mutant for the gene-encoding enzyme B would be able to grow on medium supplemented with _____. A) nutrient D only B) nutrients A and D C) nutrients B and D D) nutrients C and D E) nutrients B, C, and D F) nutrients A, B, and D G) nutrients A, C, and D

@body

- Enzyme A
- Enzyme B
- Enzyme C
- A  →  B  →  C  →  D
- Answer: D

=== layout: title-content

# Gene = Polypeptide

@body

- Many proteins are composed of several polypeptides, each of which has its own gene. Beadle and Tatum’s hypothesis is now:
  - one gene–one polypeptide
- Note that it is common to refer to gene products as proteins rather than polypeptides

=== layout: title-content

# Which of the following contradicts the one-gene, one-enzyme hypothesis? A) A mutation in a single gene can result in a defective protein. B) Alkaptonuria results when individuals lack a single enzyme involved in the catalysis of homogentisic acid. C) Sickle-cell anemia results in defective hemoglobin. D) A single antibody gene can code for different related proteins, depending on the splicing that takes place post-transcriptionally.

@body

- Answer: D

