=== layout: title-content

# What does it mean when we say the genetic code is redundant? A) A single codon (three nucleotides) can specify the addition of more than one amino acid. B) The genetic code is different for different domains of organisms. C) The genetic code is universal (the same for all organisms). D) More than one codon can specify the addition of the same amino acid.

@body

- Answer: D

=== layout: title-content

# Genetic Code

@body

- The genetic code is redundant and unambiguous
  - Redundant:
    - degenerate; being in excess; characterized by verbosity or unnecessary repetition
    - two different codons can specify the same amino acid
  - Unambiguous:
    - unequivocal; clear; indistinct; having only a single meaning or interpretation
    - no codon specifies more than one amino acid
- codon
- amino acid
- codon
- amino acid
- codon

